<!--
    Swift string literals chosen by a branch or a `csel` (`option swiftstr`).

    Issue: a Swift.String literal is two words, the count word and the object
    word, and `swiftstr` paired an object word held in a register with the
    count word in the register numbered eight bytes below it. When the words
    are picked per branch (`csel x8, x8, x9`, one select per register), that
    register holds another arm's count word: `Field.hash(into:)` printed
    "serviceIrovider" ("serviceItems"'s count word, "serviceProvider"'s
    object word), `pick` printed "animal" where it hashes "serviceItems" or
    "serviceDate", and the words of the other arms stayed raw.

    Stage / sub-stage: P5 const-pointer, `RuleSwiftString`
    (`kuna-decomp/src/p5_types/kuna_swiftstr.rs`): a register-held object word
    is paired by data flow. Every consumer that takes it beside its count word
    (a call or return input, a `PIECE`, a store eight bytes above) names the
    partner, which is traced back along the same `MULTIEQUAL` arms; two
    `MULTIEQUAL`s of different blocks correspond when their branches test the
    same condition.

    Fixture `macho_swift_strsel_arm64` (arm64 Mach-O, swiftc 6.3 -O,
    stripped; source `macho_swift_strsel.swift`): 0x100000d30 `pick` (two
    `csel` pairs per literal), 0x100000f1c `Field.hash(into:)` (a switch),
    0x100000e30 `tag` (a selected literal stored into a class field),
    0x100000eb0 `blank` (the empty literal stored). Pass 1 (`swiftstr off`)
    shows the raw words; pass 2 (the default) and pass 3 (with
    `swiftmetadata on`, whose names spell the branch conditions as separate
    negations) show each arm's own literal.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
